# Kavacha patch 0039 — Fix D0 (0034 destroyed pinned tabs) + Settings toggle
#
# !!! READ THIS FIRST — THE FEATURE IS NOT FINISHED !!!
#
# What this patch DOES fix, verified: the DATA LOSS. Pinned tabs no longer die
# on quit, sessionstore.jsonlz4 is written normally, and the TabState crash is
# gone. Confirmed on three separate binaries (2993 / 3011 / 3014 bytes written,
# both pinned tabs restored, zero TypeErrors).
#
# What is STILL BROKEN: turning the pref ON leaves the window with no browser
# element on the NEXT start. Measured on buildID 20260801161436 — Marionette
# cannot attach: `TypeError: can't access property "namespaceURI",
# browserElement is null` at driver.sys.mjs #registerBrowser. The control arm on
# the SAME binary with the pref off relaunches perfectly (5 tabs, 0 errors,
# sessionstore.jsonlz4 3031 bytes), so this is caused by the cleanup itself, not
# by the harness or by startup timing.
#
# Moving selection to a survivor before closing, and deferring a turn with
# dispatchToMainThread, did NOT fix it — so the cause is something other than
# "we closed the selected tab", and is not yet diagnosed.
#
# THEREFORE: kavacha.session.clear-unpinned-on-quit defaults to FALSE and MUST
# STAY FALSE until this is diagnosed. Do not advertise the feature, and do not
# flip the default. See documentation/BLOCKED.md B6 — whether to finish 0034 or
# drop it is an open product decision.
#
# WHAT: patch 0034 shipped a feature that DESTROYED PINNED TABS on every quit.
# This rewrites its mechanism, and adds the Settings checkbox it never had.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# D0 — why 0034's approach could never have worked
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 0034 observed "quit-application-granted" and called gBrowser.removeTab() on
# every unpinned tab, on the theory that "SessionStore keeps its in-memory state
# live as tabs close, so the final write already excludes them". That theory is
# wrong on three counts, all read out of engine/:
#
#  1. SessionStore registers its observers in init(), before any browser window
#     exists (SessionStore.sys.mjs:1310). Its "quit-application-granted" handler
#     therefore always runs FIRST and snapshots every window via
#     _collectWindowData(). Kavacha's observer, registered per-window from
#     ZenStartup, always runs after that snapshot.
#  2. getCurrentState() re-collects window data only `if (RunState.isRunning)`
#     (SessionStore.sys.mjs:5630). Once shutdown begins the state that will be
#     written is frozen. Removing tabs after the snapshot cannot influence what
#     is saved.
#  3. Removing them actively CORRUPTS the save. The removed tab elements remain
#     in gZenWorkspaces.allStoredTabs with a null linkedBrowser, and the next
#     _collectWindowData() dereferences `browser.audioMuted` with no null guard
#     (TabState.sys.mjs:79). Because _collectWindowData assigns
#     `winData.tabs = []` BEFORE its loop, the throw leaves the tab array
#     truncated and aborts the shutdown save entirely.
#
# Net effect: sessionstore.jsonlz4 is never produced, so the next start restores
# nothing and every pinned tab is gone. The ~2 KB "pinned-only" recovery.jsonlz4
# that made this look half-working is not the feature succeeding — it is the
# crash truncating the array mid-loop, with the pinned tabs simply collected
# first.
#
# Measured on buildID 20260731235720, fresh profile, full process-exit waits:
#   pref ON  (3 runs): NO sessionstore.jsonlz4; recovery 2018/2024/2018 bytes;
#                      exactly 2 TabState TypeErrors per quit
#   pref OFF (control): sessionstore.jsonlz4 3010 bytes; ZERO errors
#
# Moving the work earlier, to "quit-application-requested", does NOT fix it:
# nsAppStartup::Quit fires "requested" and "granted" in one synchronous
# sequence, so the detached tabs are still there when SessionStore collects.
# Measured on buildID 20260801085833: 4 TypeErrors, still no sessionstore file,
# and startup parsed zero windows. There is no safe point during shutdown.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The fix
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Do the work at STARTUP instead. KavachaSessionCleanup now awaits
# SessionStore.promiseAllWindowsRestored and closes the non-pinned tabs the
# session just restored. The user-visible contract is unchanged — after a quit
# you get only your pinned tabs — but nothing touches SessionStore during
# shutdown, so the session file is written normally and pinned tabs survive.
#
# It awaits the PROMISE rather than observing "sessionstore-windows-restored",
# and that distinction is load-bearing. The notification is a one-shot, and
# init() runs from ZenStartup, which can be late: measured on buildID
# 20260801151610, the observer registered AFTER the notification had already
# fired, so the cleanup never ran. That build fixed the data loss but left the
# feature a silent no-op — pinned tabs survived, and so did every unpinned one.
# The promise resolves at exactly the same three sites the notification fires
# (SessionStore.sys.mjs:2149/2177/7817 vs :2144/2171/7815) and can be awaited
# after the fact, so it cannot be missed however init() is ordered.
#
# This is cheap: browser.sessionstore.restore_on_demand defaults to true
# (firefox.js:1491), so restored background tabs are lazy and are discarded
# before they ever hit the network. They land in the normal "recently closed"
# list, so a mistake is undoable. A window that restored ONLY unpinned tabs
# keeps its last tab rather than closing into an empty window.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# D0b — the pref existed nowhere
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# kavacha.session.clear-unpinned-on-quit was declared in NO prefs file:
# getPrefType() returned 0 (PREF_INVALID) and the destructive path was on solely
# because of the hardcoded `true` fallback in getBoolPref(PREF, true). It is now
# declared in ui/defaults/kavacha-ux.js (that part of the change is outside this
# patch, in the Kavacha repo proper) and defaults to FALSE — this is the only
# Kavacha behaviour that discards user data on an ordinary action, so it is
# opt-in. The code default here matches.
#
# This patch adds the Settings UI it never had: a "Tabs kept after quitting"
# group in the Workspaces pane with checkbox kavachaWorkspacesClearUnpinned,
# wired with the same live pref observer the 0038 checkbox uses, so it reflects
# an externally-set pref.
#
# Touches: src/zen/common/sys/KavachaSessionCleanup.sys.mjs (rewrite),
# src/browser/components/preferences/kavachaWorkspaces.inc.xhtml (new groupbox),
# src/browser/components/preferences/kavacha-workspaces.js (wiring),
# locales/en-US/browser/browser/preferences/zen-preferences.ftl (5 strings).
#
# Two further traps this fix had to clear, both found by measurement:
#
#  * Awaiting the promise means init() ordering no longer matters, but the
#    cleanup then runs from INSIDE SessionStore's restore path. Closing tabs
#    synchronously from there races its bookkeeping, so the work is deferred one
#    turn with Services.tm.dispatchToMainThread.
#  * Closing the restored SELECTED tab mid-startup can leave the window with no
#    linked browser. Observed on buildID 20260801154708 as Marionette refusing
#    to attach: `TypeError: can't access property "namespaceURI", browserElement
#    is null` -- a window the user would find broken. Selection is now moved to
#    a surviving tab BEFORE anything is closed, and the whole pass is skipped if
#    no survivor exists.
